TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup coverage",
    "organizer",
    "shaper",
    "terminal",
    "within-team variation",
    "talent control",
    "role coverage separability"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define and measure lineup coverage reliably from play-type possession records?",
    "How to test within-team variation of coverage?",
    "How to separate coverage effect from overall talent and simple role counts?",
    "How to control for player talent when testing coverage?",
    "What are potential confounders (e.g., opponent strength, game context) and how to address them?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No prior project studies were retrieved for this specific question, so nothing is established."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "The feasibility of reliably defining organizer/shaper/terminal roles from the available data.",
    "The magnitude of lineup-coverage variation within teams.",
    "The separability of coverage effects from overall talent and role counts.",
    "The behavior of talent controls in this context."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question can be sharpened into a testable form: define roles via clustering of play-type distributions from past seasons; define coverage as a binary indicator per lineup (or per stint) of having at least one player in each role; estimate the effect of coverage on offensive/defensive efficiency within team, controlling for player fixed effects or rolling player quality metrics.",
    "Within-team variation in coverage is plausible because injuries, substitutions, and tactical adjustments change role availability.",
    "Separability from talent can be tested by including player-level quality ratings or past performance metrics in the model.",
    "Potential endogeneity must be addressed: lineups are not randomly assigned, and coverage may correlate with game situation."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether the organizer/shaper/terminal taxonomy maps cleanly onto NBA play-type data; the roles may overlap or be ambiguous for many players.",
    "Whether sufficient within-team variation exists across the 90 team-seasons and 420,993 possessions.",
    "Whether the talent control fully addresses confounding from coaching decisions and matchup effects.",
    "Whether the outcome (e.g., points per possession) is measured at the appropriate level (possession, stint, or game).",
    "Potential look-ahead bias if role definitions use data from the same games tested."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior studies were retrieved, so the decision was based solely on the current task's data and methodological assessment."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The role definitions (organizer, shaper, terminal) must be reliable and stable across seasons; otherwise coverage measurement is noisy and may produce spurious results."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If most lineups already contain at least one of each role, the exposure variation may be too small to estimate effects with adequate power."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior studies by this project on lineup coverage with organizer/shaper/terminal roles were returned."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The classification of players into organizer, shaper, terminal roles from play-type data may be unreliable if the taxonomy is not well-defined or if many players exhibit hybrid profiles."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "If the condition '>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal' is nearly always satisfied in NBA lineups, coverage will lack variation, making an effect test underpowered."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "If coverage is very common, the base rate may be too high to identify a meaningful effect, or the comparison group may be too small."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Coaching decisions, opponent matchups, game situation, and correlated player quality can confound the coverage-performance relationship."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If roles are derived from the same game data used to test coverage effects, look-ahead bias may inflate the apparent effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Lineup changes are often reactive to performance; if a lineup underperforms, a substitution may alter coverage, creating reverse causality."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing multiple outcomes (e.g., offensive rating, defensive rating, net rating) and sub-group analyses could inflate false-positive risk."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Although the overall possession count is large, the effective sample at the unique-lineup or stint level may be limited, particularly for rare coverage states."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.15
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The within-team effect of lineup coverage (binary: >=1 player classified as organizer, >=1 as shaper, >=1 as terminal) on points scored per 100 possessions (offensive rating) and points allowed per 100 possessions (defensive rating), holding constant player quality.",
    "unit": "Stint (a continuous segment of a game where neither team changes personnel), or possession-level, depending on role assignment stability.",
    "sample": "All regular-season games from 2022-23 through 2025-26, excluding garbage time (defined as game in last 5 minutes with point differential >20, or coach's challenge situations).",
    "power": "Simulation-based power analysis using pilot data from the earliest season (2022-23) to detect a 1.5-point per 100 possessions effect, assuming an intra-team coverage variation prevalence of at least 15% of stints.",
    "controls": "Player fixed effects (or random effects if crossed), team-season fixed effects, opponent strength (opponent defensive/offensive rating from prior games), rest days, home/away, and a rolling player quality metric (e.g., RAPM or simple plus-minus over the previous 20 games) as a continuous talent control.",
    "falsification": "Test whether coverage affects outcomes that should be unaffected (e.g., free-throw percentage of the opponent, or next-game performance of the same players). Also, test for reverse causality by examining whether prior stint performance predicts subsequent coverage changes.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-specify primary outcomes (offensive rating and net rating), use Benjamini-Hochberg correction for any exploratory sub-analyses, and report all specifications in a pre-analysis plan.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis of no coverage effect if the coverage coefficient's 95% confidence interval excludes zero and the effect is stable across multiple role-definition sensitivity analyses (e.g., stricter/looser role assignment thresholds).",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular-season data as available on 2026-07-13; roles defined using a hold-out season (2022-23) to avoid look-ahead."
  }
}

